// src/kernel/core/acpi/ec_event.pdx — R31.M1-004 (#1092) + R31.M1-005 (#1093)
//
// What an embedded-controller query byte MEANS, and which meanings are
// allowed to be lost when the stream is full.
//
// =============================================================================
// §0  power_policy_channel DOES NOT EXIST, AND THIS MODULE DOES NOT CREATE IT
// =============================================================================
//
// #1092's deliverable reads "AC insertion/removal event ->
// power_policy_channel". Before anything was designed, that name was
// looked for. It occurs EXACTLY ONCE in this repository —
// `design/roadmap/next-wave-softarch.md` line 140 — as a name in a
// sentence, with no code, no schema, no kind and no caller. It is the
// same shape `acpi_event_channel` had when #1091 went looking for it one
// iteration ago.
//
// So the decision, stated once here and repeated in
// `design/drivers/embedded-controller-events.md` §1: THERE IS NO SECOND
// CHANNEL. AC events, lid events, power-button presses and hot-keys all
// travel the path #1091 built — `ec_route_query` -> `acpi_evt_offer` with
// `ACPI_EVT_SRC_EC_QUERY` — and this module supplies the one thing that
// path was missing, which is MEANING.
//
// Two event channels with overlapping purpose would be worse than one for
// a reason that is specific rather than aesthetic: the stream's
// accounting identity (`offered == drained + depth + drops + unrouted`)
// is what makes a lost platform event localisable, and a second channel
// carrying a subset of the same events would give an operator two
// incomplete sequences and no way to interleave them. #1091 added the
// stream's third source precisely because the R30.M4 design anticipated
// extension; taking that invitation is cheaper and more honest than
// declining it.
//
// =============================================================================
// §1  WHAT READS THESE EVENTS: NOTHING. SAID PLAINLY.
// =============================================================================
//
// A routing mechanism whose events nobody reads is a mechanism with no
// subject. So, exactly:
//
//   * No ring-3 program binds a KIND_ACPI_EVENT endpoint. Every call site
//     of `acpi_evt_bind` is in a boot witness; nothing under `src/user/`
//     references any `acpi_evt_*` symbol at all.
//   * There is no DRAIN op and no ACK op on KIND_ACPI_EVENT, by design
//     rather than by omission: a record is 64 bytes and a three-register
//     capability invocation cannot copy one out without taking a
//     destination pointer, which is the aliasing the `_acpi_evt_ring`
//     confinement assertion exists to prevent
//     (design/kernel/r30-m4-sci-gpe-path.md §11.6).
//   * There is no power policy, no lid handler, no battery monitor and no
//     platform shutdown consumer anywhere under `src/user/`.
//   * `aml_ec_query_pump` — the ring-3 half that would CALL
//     `ec_route_query` — has no caller in `src/` either. Its only callers
//     are in the C harness.
//
// THEREFORE: THE CONSUMER OF EVERYTHING IN THIS MODULE IS A BOOT WITNESS,
// AND THE PRESS OF A POWER BUTTON STILL DOES NOT SHUT THIS MACHINE DOWN.
// The two fingerprints in `tests/kernel/cap/ec_query_synth.pdx` assert
// what actually happens — a classified record lands in a kernel ring, the
// shed rule holds, a loss is counted against its class — and assert
// NOTHING about delivery to a policy layer, because there is no policy
// layer to deliver to.
//
// What this iteration removes is a different absence, and it is a real
// one. Before it, a decoded query byte had no meaning anywhere in the
// system: nothing in the tree could say that 0x2B is a lid transition and
// 0x20 is the power button, so "route AC insertion to power policy" was
// not merely unimplemented, it was INEXPRESSIBLE. After it, the meaning
// exists, is authority-gated, is monotone, and is carried in the record.
//
// =============================================================================
// §2  THE MAP IS INSTALLED, NOT COMPILED IN
// =============================================================================
//
// There is no correct table of query-byte meanings that can be written
// here. `_Qxx` numbering is chosen by each vendor's firmware: what a
// method DOES is expressed as the AML body of `_Q2B`, and the only way to
// learn that 0x2B is the lid is to see that its body notifies the lid
// device. That is bytecode evaluation, and `design/acpi/
// no-aml-in-kernel.md` forbids it in ring 0 down to the token level.
//
// So the kernel holds a table of small integers and never learns them by
// itself. `ec_event_map_set` is the installer, and its intended caller is
// the ring-3 supervisor that already evaluates the controller's methods.
// Hardcoding the T14 G4's numbering here would have produced a kernel
// that silently misreads every other laptop, which is worse than a kernel
// that admits it does not know.
//
// =============================================================================
// §3  THE MAP STARTS EMPTY, ONLY FILLS, AND A FILLED ENTRY CANNOT CHANGE
// =============================================================================
//
// The notify bitmap in `core/cap/kind_ec_query.pdx` starts FULL and only
// ever narrows. This table is its mirror image: it starts EMPTY and only
// ever fills, and an entry that has a meaning cannot acquire a different
// one while the map is bound.
//
// That invariant is the most important thing in this file. The failure it
// forbids is a byte that means POWER at boot and HOTKEY an hour later:
// every downstream decision — whether the event may be shed under
// pressure, which counter a loss lands in, what a subscriber does when it
// arrives — is taken from the class, so a silently remapped byte
// reclassifies a power-button press as a droppable Fn key and there is no
// symptom until somebody's machine will not turn off.
//
// Re-installing a byte with the SAME class returns OK, because a caller
// that installs the map twice has achieved what it asked for. Installing
// a DIFFERENT class returns EC_EVT_MAP_ALREADY and is counted. Wholesale
// clearing exists only in `ec_event_reset` (boot and teardown) and in
// `ec_event_map_unbind`, and neither is a per-entry operation — the
// monotonicity claimed is monotonicity WITHIN A BINDING, which is the
// strongest form that is meaningful for a table whose contents are true
// only of one controller.
//
// `tools/build.sh` greps for the absence of the natural names a demotion
// primitive would be given, the same way it greps for a widening
// primitive on the notify bitmap.
//
// =============================================================================
// §4  ONE MAP, ONE CONTROLLER, AND THE ASSUMPTION IS ENFORCED
// =============================================================================
//
// The table is 256 entries, keyed by query byte — not by (controller,
// byte), which would be 65536. The T14 G4 has one embedded controller and
// so does every machine this OS has been near.
//
// A single-controller assumption left implicit is how a second controller
// silently inherits the first one's meanings. So the map is BOUND to one
// controller address, inherited from a KIND_OP_REGION capability rather
// than accepted as an argument, and `ec_event_class_of` returns
// EC_EVT_CLASS_NONE for any other address — and COUNTS it. A second
// controller therefore gets no meanings rather than the wrong ones, which
// fails toward "the events are unclassified" instead of toward "the
// events are misclassified", and the counter says which happened.
//
// `ec_event_map_bind` has ARITY ONE for the reason `ec_access_bind` does:
// a caller-supplied controller address would let a subscription's
// meanings be attached to a machine the capability never named.
//
// =============================================================================
// §5  ONE ENTRY PER QWORD, WHICH IS 2 KiB OF .bss AND WORTH IT
// =============================================================================
//
// A class fits in three bits and eight would fit per qword. They are not
// packed, for two reasons.
//
// First, packing makes installing one byte a READ-MODIFY-WRITE OVER SEVEN
// NEIGHBOURS. On a table where one neighbour is the power button and
// another is a hot-key, a mask bug does not corrupt the entry being
// written — it corrupts a different event's meaning, which is the class
// of bug §3 exists to prevent and the hardest kind to see.
//
// Second, the mnemonic table has no variable-count shift (see
// `ec_query_row_wants`, which walks a 6-bit count one bit at a time and
// says why). Packed access would need two of those bounded loops per
// lookup on the routing path.
//
// Unpacked, an index is `byte << 3` and an entry is a whole qword nothing
// else shares. Two kilobytes of .bss is not a quantity this kernel needs
// to argue about.
//
// =============================================================================
// §6  EVENT LOSS — CAN IT DROP, WHAT HAPPENS WHEN FULL, IS IT DETECTABLE
// =============================================================================
//
// The `QR_EC` mechanism has no replay. The controller signals, the OS
// asks, the controller answers with one query byte, and if that byte is
// not drained the event is simply gone. So the three questions have to be
// answered rather than assumed, and the answers are:
//
// CAN THE PATH DROP AN EVENT?  YES, in three distinct ways, and each one
// has its own counter and its own code:
//
//   1. THE STREAM IS FULL. `acpi_evt_offer` is TAIL-DROP at depth 32: it
//      refuses the new record and keeps the old ones. `ec_route_query`
//      counts that in `refused`, and this module counts it against the
//      event's CLASS as `lost`.
//   2. NOTHING IS BOUND, or no subscription wants the byte. Counted as
//      `unrouted` and `filtered` respectively by the router. Nothing was
//      lost that anybody had asked for.
//   3. THE ADMISSION RULE SHED IT. §7. Counted against the class as
//      `shed`, and the router returns EC_ROUTE_SHED rather than a routed
//      count of zero, so a shed event and an unwanted one are never the
//      same observation.
//
// WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE DESTINATION IS FULL OR ABSENT?  The offer is
// refused, the offer NUMBER is still consumed, and the record never
// exists. Refusal is the whole of it: nothing blocks, nothing retries,
// and no old record is overwritten. A caller that wants to know pumped
// harder has the return code.
//
// IS A DROPPED EVENT DETECTABLE?  YES, AND PER CLASS, WHICH IS THE POINT.
// The stream's own `drops` counter says "some platform event was lost".
// That is not enough: a lost hot-key is a keystroke and a lost power
// button is an unclean shutdown, and an aggregate cannot tell them apart.
// `ec_event_stat(EC_EVT_CLASS_POWER, EC_EVT_CT_LOST)` can, and the
// witness asserts it moves. NOTHING ON THE POWER-BUTTON PATH IS DROPPED
// SILENTLY.
//
// The one gap stated rather than closed: the ring-3 pump that would call
// the router does not exist yet, so a query lost BEFORE `ec_route_query`
// — because nobody issued `QR_EC` at all — is invisible here and would
// have to be counted in the pump. §1 is why that pump is absent.
//
// =============================================================================
// §7  THE PRIORITY INVERSION THIS MILESTONE WOULD OTHERWISE HAVE SHIPPED
// =============================================================================
//
// Fn keys arrive as BURSTS. One press of a brightness key can produce
// several `_Qxx` queries in rapid succession, and holding it produces a
// stream of them. Everything else on this path is a singleton: the lid
// closes once, the adapter is pulled once, the power button is pressed
// once.
//
// The stream is 32 deep, shared with GPE and firmware-notification
// traffic, and TAIL-DROP. Compose those two facts:
//
//   A LONG ENOUGH HOT-KEY BURST FILLS THE STREAM, AND THE NEXT RECORD
//   REFUSED IS WHATEVER ARRIVES NEXT — INCLUDING THE POWER BUTTON.
//
// That is a priority inversion in which the least important event on the
// path evicts the most important one, and it is not a hypothetical: it is
// what a correct tail-drop ring does when one producer is bursty and the
// consumer is a policy process that has not been scheduled yet. Holding
// the brightness key while pressing power would be the reproduction.
//
// THE RULE. A HOTKEY-class query is refused admission once the stream's
// depth reaches EC_EVT_HOTKEY_WATERMARK (24 of 32), leaving eight slots
// that a hot-key burst cannot consume. AC, lid, power-button and thermal
// events are never shed and may use the whole ring.
//
// R31.M2-002 (#1095) CHANGED THE SHAPE OF THAT SENTENCE WITHOUT CHANGING
// ITS CONTENT. #1093 expressed the rule as one inequality — `class !=
// EC_EVT_CLASS_HOTKEY` — and defended it against a range compare on the
// grounds that a range would sweep this milestone's thermal class into the
// watermark check the moment it was declared. The defence was right, and
// when the class landed nothing had to change. But an inequality can only
// say WHICH CLASS IS SHEDDABLE; it cannot say WHICH CLASSES WERE
// CONSIDERED, so every future class arrives admitted by a default nobody
// had to look at. That default is right for thermal and battery, both
// singletons, and WRONG for R31.M4's platform sensors — a sample stream
// consuming the ring the power button shares is §7's own inversion
// arriving from the other side, past the very rule that prevents it.
//
// So the rule is now TWO ENUMERATIONS AND A HOLE, in
// `ec_event_shed_policy`: SHED_WATERMARK = {HOTKEY}, SHED_ALWAYS =
// {NONE, AC, LID, POWER, THERMAL}, each member reached by an equality
// against its own name, and no ordering relation anywhere. A class in
// neither set answers SHED_UNDECLARED and is ADMITTED — fail-safe,
// because refusing an unrecognised class drops exactly the safety event
// whose class somebody forgot to declare. What makes the hole unreachable
// is not the runtime default but `tools/build.sh`, which reads the class
// declarations in this file and refuses a build in which any of them
// lacks a `policy:` marker in the policy body. The next class is admitted
// or shed by a decision; it cannot be admitted by omission.
//
// Why 24, and why a watermark rather than a quota:
//
//   * EIGHT RESERVED IS MORE THAN THE NON-SHEDDABLE CLASSES CAN
//     SIMULTANEOUSLY NEED. There are three of them, the controller
//     answers one query at a time, and a machine with more than eight
//     un-drained lid-and-power events has a wedged consumer rather than a
//     backlog.
//   * A WATERMARK NEEDS ONLY `acpi_evt_depth`. A per-class quota would
//     have to be decremented when a record is drained, which would couple
//     this rule to `acpi_evt_pop` — a function that deliberately knows
//     nothing about classes, and that in a real boot nothing calls.
//   * SHEDDING IS NOT COALESCING and this module does not coalesce.
//     Collapsing repeated hot-keys would mean scanning the pending
//     records, i.e. reaching into `_acpi_evt_ring` from outside the one
//     object allowed to relocate against it. Dropping a hot-key that
//     arrives past the watermark, AND COUNTING IT, is a worse user
//     experience and a much better boundary, and the count is what tells
//     an operator to repair the consumer rather than the ring.
//
// THE RESERVATION IS SOFT AND SAYING SO MATTERS. The eight slots are
// reserved against HOT-KEYS ONLY. GPE records and firmware notifications
// share this stream and are not shed, so a GPE storm can still fill the
// ring and refuse a power-button press — which the per-class `lost`
// counter then records. Making the reservation hard would mean teaching
// `acpi_evt_offer` about classes, and it has two other sources whose
// records carry none.
//
// =============================================================================
// §8  THE FIVE CLASSES, AND WHY EACH ONE IS ON THIS LIST
// =============================================================================
//
// THE POWER BUTTON (EC_EVT_CLASS_POWER). A long press is handled by
// firmware and the OS never sees it. So the OS path is the ONLY GRACEFUL
// ONE: a lost short-press means the machine does not shut down when
// asked, and the user's next move is to hold the button, which is an
// unclean shutdown every single time. Never shed. Its losses are counted
// separately from every other class.
//
// LID (EC_EVT_CLASS_LID). On a laptop a close normally means suspend, and
// on this target the EC owns fan control — so a missed lid event leaves a
// running machine in a closed bag, which is a THERMAL event and not
// merely a power one. Never shed.
//
// AC PRESENCE (EC_EVT_CLASS_AC). Insertion and removal drive charging
// policy. A missed removal leaves the system believing it is on wall
// power while the battery drains. One class for both directions: which
// direction it was is the `_Qxx` byte, which the record carries, and
// firmware numbers the two transitions separately or shares one method
// depending on the vendor — a class that tried to encode the direction
// would be a claim about firmware this kernel cannot check.
//
// HOT-KEYS (EC_EVT_CLASS_HOTKEY). The only bursty class, the only
// sheddable one, and the reason §7 exists.
//
// THERMAL (EC_EVT_CLASS_THERMAL) — added by R31.M2-002 (#1095). A `_Qxx`
// byte whose method notifies a ThermalZone: a trip crossing. NEVER SHED,
// and unlike the three classes above it, its non-sheddability is now
// stated by NAME rather than inherited from a default — see §7's policy
// function. A lost `_CRT` crossing on a machine whose EC owns fan control
// is not a missed notification, it is a part running past its limit with
// nothing watching, and there is no user gesture that recovers it the way
// holding the power button recovers a lost short press.
//
// BATTERY IS STILL DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED HERE. R31.M3 owns those
// events and will extend the enumeration; a class value that nothing
// produces and nothing consumes would be a constant pretending to be a
// mechanism. `ec_event_shed_policy` answers EC_EVT_SHED_UNDECLARED for
// every value above EC_EVT_CLASS_MAX, and `tools/build.sh` refuses a
// build in which a DECLARED class has no policy row — so the next class
// is admitted or shed by a decision, never by a default.
//
// =============================================================================
// §9  NO PORT I/O, NO INTERRUPT CONTEXT
// =============================================================================
//
// This module contains no `in`/`out`; `tools/build.sh` asserts that port
// I/O under `core/acpi/` is confined to `gpe_io.o` and this object is
// swept by that check automatically. It is not on `SCI_ISR_ALLOWED` and
// must not be: `ec_event_admit` reads the stream's depth and the map
// lookup is a load, both bounded, but the routing path they serve is
// task-context work reached through the existing deferral
// (ISR -> ring -> pump -> task).

module EcEvent = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // §8's enumeration.
  //
  // NONE is 0 and means "this kernel does not know what this byte means",
  // which is a legitimate steady state — an unmapped byte still ROUTES,
  // because a subscriber that knows the platform can act on the byte
  // itself and refusing to deliver an event because the kernel could not
  // label it would be the kernel withholding data it does not own.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let EC_EVT_CLASS_NONE   : u64 = 0
  pub let EC_EVT_CLASS_AC     : u64 = 1   // #1092
  pub let EC_EVT_CLASS_LID    : u64 = 2   // #1093
  pub let EC_EVT_CLASS_POWER  : u64 = 3   // #1093 — the power button
  pub let EC_EVT_CLASS_HOTKEY : u64 = 4   // #1093 — Fn keys
  pub let EC_EVT_CLASS_THERMAL : u64 = 5  // #1095 — a thermal-zone trip
  pub let EC_EVT_CLASS_MAX    : u64 = 5

  // Number of class slots the counter block holds: NONE..THERMAL.
  // _ec_evt_stats is [u64; 32] and each class owns four counters, so the
  // block bound is 8 classes; R31.M3's battery class is the seventh.
  pub let EC_EVT_CLASSES : u64 = 6

  // ==========================================================================
  // §7's admission policy, as a TOTAL function over the class enumeration.
  //
  // Two named sets and a third state for "no decision was taken", because
  // neither default is safe for every class this enumeration will grow:
  // defaulting to sheddable loses a safety event, and defaulting to
  // unconditional lets a future high-rate class (R31.M4's platform sensors
  // are the concrete risk) fill a 32-slot tail-drop ring that the power
  // button shares. The runtime default is the fail-safe one — UNDECLARED
  // is admitted — and the real guard is at build time, where
  // tools/build.sh refuses a declared class with no policy row.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let EC_EVT_SHED_ALWAYS     : u64 = 0  // admitted unconditionally
  pub let EC_EVT_SHED_WATERMARK  : u64 = 1  // subject to §7's watermark
  pub let EC_EVT_SHED_UNDECLARED : u64 = 2  // not a member of either set

  // ==========================================================================
  // Failure taxonomy — 0xFFFFFDB0..BF.
  //
  // A sixteen-wide band disjoint from every other in tree: registry_v2
  // owns 0xFFFFFD0x, the 0xFFFFFD2x/4x/5x/6x/8x/Ax/Cx blocks are taken,
  // and core/acpi/ec_route.pdx owns 0xFFFFFDEx. 0xFFFFFDBx was free.
  // Disjointness is what lets a caller that threads a code up through the
  // router and the stream still name the gate that refused it.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let EC_EVT_OK              : u64 = 0
  pub let EC_EVT_MAP_UNBOUND     : u64 = 0xFFFFFDBF
  pub let EC_EVT_MAP_BAD_BYTE    : u64 = 0xFFFFFDBE
  pub let EC_EVT_MAP_BAD_CLASS   : u64 = 0xFFFFFDBD
  pub let EC_EVT_MAP_ALREADY     : u64 = 0xFFFFFDBC
  pub let EC_EVT_BIND_BAD_CAP    : u64 = 0xFFFFFDBB
  pub let EC_EVT_BIND_ALREADY    : u64 = 0xFFFFFDBA

  // ACPI 6.5 §12.2: the query byte domain is the 256 values of the
  // EmbeddedControl space, and byte 0 is not one of them (§12.9 — QR_EC
  // returning 0 means no query is pending).
  pub let EC_EVT_BYTE_MAX : u64 = 256

  // §7's watermark: hot-keys are refused admission at this depth, of the
  // stream's 32 slots.
  pub let EC_EVT_HOTKEY_WATERMARK : u64 = 24

  // ==========================================================================
  // Storage.
  //
  // _ec_evt_map      256 entries, one class per query byte, index byte<<3.
  //
  // _ec_evt_owner    +0   bound      0 | 1
  //                  +8   ec_addr    the controller these meanings are of
  //                  +16  installed  entries given a non-NONE class
  //                  +24  refused    installs rejected for any reason
  //                  +32  foreign    class lookups for another controller
  //
  // _ec_evt_stats    EC_EVT_CLASSES blocks of four counters, at
  //                  class*32 + which*8:
  //                    0 seen       queries of this class presented
  //                    1 delivered  RECORDS of this class the stream took
  //                    2 lost       offers of this class the stream refused
  //                    3 shed       queries of this class refused admission
  //
  // `seen` counts QUERIES and `delivered` counts RECORDS, so two
  // subscriptions to one controller make delivered advance by two on one
  // seen. That is not a discrepancy to be reconciled away: one is how
  // often the machine did something, the other is how many principals
  // were told, and an operator needs both.
  //
  // tools/build.sh asserts that no object other than this one relocates
  // against any of the three.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut _ec_evt_map   : [u64; 256] = uninit @align(64)
  pub let mut _ec_evt_owner : [u64; 8]   = uninit @align(64)
  pub let mut _ec_evt_stats : [u64; 32]  = uninit @align(64)

  pub let EC_EVT_OWN_BOUND     : u64 = 0
  pub let EC_EVT_OWN_ADDR      : u64 = 1
  pub let EC_EVT_OWN_INSTALLED : u64 = 2
  pub let EC_EVT_OWN_REFUSED   : u64 = 3
  pub let EC_EVT_OWN_FOREIGN   : u64 = 4

  pub let EC_EVT_CT_SEEN      : u64 = 0
  pub let EC_EVT_CT_DELIVERED : u64 = 1
  pub let EC_EVT_CT_LOST      : u64 = 2
  pub let EC_EVT_CT_SHED      : u64 = 3
  pub let EC_EVT_CT_MAX       : u64 = 4

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_reset() -> ()
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_reset : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): clear the meaning table, the binding and every per-class counter. Boot and teardown only. This is the ONE wholesale clear of the table and it is not a violation of the monotonicity in sec 3: what that invariant forbids is an entry acquiring a second meaning while the map is bound, and this runs when nothing is bound and no subscriber can be reading. Three separate store runs rather than one, because the three arrays have different lengths and a single loop over their sum would encode their adjacency in .bss as a fact the code depends on. The map loop is 256 iterations, bounded by an immediate compare. Leaf. Labels prefixed ec_evt_rs_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_map];
        xor rax, rax;
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ec_evt_rs_map:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 256;
        jb  ec_evt_rs_map;

        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ec_evt_rs_own:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 8;
        jb  ec_evt_rs_own;

        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_stats];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ec_evt_rs_stat:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 32;
        jb  ec_evt_rs_stat;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // Observers.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_map_bound : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): 1 iff a controller's meanings are installed against this map. Read by the witness and by ec_event_map_set's own gate. Leaf.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let ec_event_map_addr : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): the controller address this map's meanings are true of, 0 when unbound. Inherited at bind from a KIND_OP_REGION row and never accepted as an argument, so this is a report of what the capability said rather than of what a caller claimed. Leaf.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let ec_event_own_stat : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (index: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): bounds-checked read of the binding block; an index >= 8 returns 0 rather than reading past the array. Zero is safe as the out-of-range answer for the reason acpi_evt_peek's zero is safe: this is an observation channel with no authority behind it, and a caller asking for a counter that does not exist has learned nothing it could act on. Leaf. Labels prefixed ec_evt_os_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 8;
        jae ec_evt_os_zero;
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        add r8, r10;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        ret;
      ec_evt_os_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  pub let ec_event_stat : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (class: u64, which: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-005 (#1093): bounds-checked read of one per-class counter. class >= EC_EVT_CLASSES or which >= EC_EVT_CT_MAX returns 0. THIS IS THE FUNCTION THAT MAKES A DROPPED POWER-BUTTON EVENT DETECTABLE: the stream's aggregate `drops` says a platform event was lost, and only this can say it was the power button rather than the twenty-fifth Fn key of a burst. Offset is class*32 + which*8, both constant shifts. Leaf. Labels prefixed ec_evt_st_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 6;                      // EC_EVT_CLASSES
        jae ec_evt_st_zero;
        cmp rsi, 4;                      // EC_EVT_CT_MAX
        jae ec_evt_st_zero;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 5;                       // class * 32 bytes
        mov r9, rsi;
        shl r9, 3;
        add r8, r9;
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_stats];
        add r8, r10;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        ret;
      ec_evt_st_zero:
        xor rax, rax;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_note(class, which) -> ()
  //
  // Bump one per-class counter. The router's only write into this module.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_note : (u64, u64) -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn (class: u64, which: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-005 (#1093): increment one per-class counter; a silent no-op on an out-of-range class or counter index. A no-op rather than a refusal because the sole caller is core/acpi/ec_route.pdx's routing loop, which obtained the class from ec_event_class_of and therefore cannot present an out-of-range one -- the bound is here so that the array cannot be walked past even if that ever stops being true, not so the router can handle an error it has no way to act on. One counter per (class, which) pair rather than one aggregate with a class field, because the question these answer is always about ONE class ('was a power-button event lost') and never about the distribution. Leaf. Labels prefixed ec_evt_nt_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 6;                      // EC_EVT_CLASSES
        jae ec_evt_nt_out;
        cmp rsi, 4;                      // EC_EVT_CT_MAX
        jae ec_evt_nt_out;
        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 5;
        mov r9, rsi;
        shl r9, 3;
        add r8, r9;
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_stats];
        add r8, r10;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r8 + 0], rax;
      ec_evt_nt_out:
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_map_bind(region_slot) -> EC_EVT_OK | EC_EVT_BIND_*
  //
  // ARITY ONE. The controller address is inherited, never argued.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_map_bind : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{cap} =
    fn (region_slot: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): claim this meaning table for one embedded controller, proving the claim with a KIND_OP_REGION capability over the EmbeddedControl space. Gates, in order: (1) not already bound -> EC_EVT_BIND_ALREADY. Rebinding is REFUSED rather than replacing, for a sharper reason than acpi_evt_bind's: a silent replacement would leave the previous controller's meanings installed under the new controller's address, which is precisely the misclassification sec 4 exists to prevent, and it would do it without a single counter moving. (2) ec_query_check_region_ec(region_slot), whose gate is reused verbatim rather than reimplemented -- it already establishes a live region capability holding RIGHT_MINT, over address space 0x03, of a declared length in 1..256, and a second weaker definition of 'a legitimate embedded-controller region' is exactly the drift that makes two gates disagree. Its codes are collapsed into EC_EVT_BIND_BAD_CAP because at this layer they are one question ('may this caller say what a query byte means?') and the distinction between a wrong kind and a wrong space is already separately diagnosable at the mint that produced the capability. (3) INHERIT the controller address: ec_query_region_row then opregion_row_base of that row, a decode failure from either -> EC_EVT_BIND_BAD_CAP since the only way to reach it after gate 2 passed is a row that died between the two calls; a base of DECODE_BAD is refused rather than stored. THE ADDRESS IS NOT AN ARGUMENT ANYWHERE, for the reason ec_access_bind takes no base and no length: an argued address would attach one machine's event meanings to a controller the capability never named, and every classification thereafter would be confidently wrong. The map's CONTENTS are not touched here -- binding claims the table, installing fills it, and a bind that also cleared would let a rebind-after-unbind sequence look like it preserved meanings it silently discarded. Three pushes (rbx = region_slot, r12 = region row, r13 unused, making the count odd) leave rsp%16 == 0 at every one of the three nested calls. Labels prefixed ec_evt_bd_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rdi;

        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ec_evt_bd_already;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call ec_query_check_region_ec;
        cmp rax, 0;
        jne ec_evt_bd_bad;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call ec_query_region_row;
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r11;
        je  ec_evt_bd_bad;
        mov r12, rax;

        mov rdi, r12;
        call opregion_row_base;
        mov r11, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        cmp rax, r11;
        je  ec_evt_bd_bad;

        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;              // inherited ec_addr
        mov rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;              // bound

        xor rax, rax;                    // EC_EVT_OK
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      ec_evt_bd_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDBA;             // EC_EVT_BIND_ALREADY
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      ec_evt_bd_bad:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDBB;             // EC_EVT_BIND_BAD_CAP
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_map_unbind() -> ()
  //
  // Release the table. THE MEANINGS GO WITH IT.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_map_unbind : () -> () !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): drop the binding AND clear all 256 meanings, keeping the counters. THIS DIFFERS DELIBERATELY FROM acpi_evt_unbind, WHICH KEEPS ITS STATE. There, the state is counters, and discarding the drop totals at teardown would erase the evidence just as somebody starts looking for it -- so the counters survive here too, for the same reason. But the MAP is not evidence, it is a set of assertions that are true only of one controller: leaving it populated across a rebind would hand a second embedded controller the first one's meanings, which is the single failure sec 4 is built to prevent and the one that produces no symptom until a lid event turns off a machine. Unconditional rather than gated on being bound: an unbind of an unbound map is a normal, idempotent thing for a teardown path to do, and clearing an already-clear table costs 256 stores nobody is waiting on. Leaf. Labels prefixed ec_evt_ub_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [r10 + 0], rax;              // bound
        mov [r10 + 8], rax;              // ec_addr
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;             // installed

        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_map];
        xor rcx, rcx;
      ec_evt_ub_next:
        mov [r10 + rcx * 8], rax;
        add rcx, 1;
        cmp rcx, 256;
        jb  ec_evt_ub_next;
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_map_set(query_byte, class) -> EC_EVT_OK | EC_EVT_MAP_*
  //
  // THE INSTALLER. Fills one entry, and cannot change one.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_map_set : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (query_byte: u64, class: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): give one query byte one meaning. Gates, in order: (1) the map must be bound -> EC_EVT_MAP_UNBOUND, because a meaning with no controller behind it is an assertion about no machine, and the bind is where the authority to make such assertions was proved. (2) query_byte must be in 1..255 -> EC_EVT_MAP_BAD_BYTE. Byte 0 IS REFUSED A MEANING and not merely ignored: QR_EC returning 0 means no query is pending, core/acpi/ec_route.pdx refuses to route it and design/acpi/embedded-controller.md sec 9 refuses to dispatch it, so an entry for it could only ever be read by a caller that had already violated two other contracts, and admitting it would make this the one place in the path that treats 'nothing happened' as an event with semantics. (3) class must be <= EC_EVT_CLASS_MAX -> EC_EVT_MAP_BAD_CLASS; the thermal and battery classes R31.M2 and R31.M3 will add do not exist yet, and accepting a value above the enumeration would store a class no counter has a slot for. (4) THE MONOTONICITY GATE: if the entry already holds a non-NONE class, a request naming the SAME class returns OK (a caller that installs the map twice has achieved what it asked for) and a request naming a DIFFERENT one returns EC_EVT_MAP_ALREADY and is counted. That is the invariant of sec 3 and the most load-bearing refusal in this file -- a byte that means POWER at boot and HOTKEY an hour later reclassifies a power-button press as a droppable Fn key, and there is no symptom until a machine will not turn off. A class of NONE is accepted as a no-op on an unset entry rather than refused, so a caller sweeping all 256 bytes with whatever its parser produced does not have to filter. `installed` counts entries that acquired a non-NONE meaning, which is the vacuity guard the witness uses to prove the map was actually populated. Leaf, no nested calls. Labels prefixed ec_evt_ms_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  ec_evt_ms_unbound;

        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  ec_evt_ms_bad_byte;
        mov r11, 256;                    // EC_EVT_BYTE_MAX
        cmp rdi, r11;
        jae ec_evt_ms_bad_byte;

        mov r11, 5;                      // EC_EVT_CLASS_MAX
        cmp rsi, r11;
        ja  ec_evt_ms_bad_class;

        mov r8, rdi;
        shl r8, 3;
        lea r9, [rip + _ec_evt_map];
        add r8, r9;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  ec_evt_ms_store;             // unset: fill it
        cmp rax, rsi;
        je  ec_evt_ms_same;              // same meaning: idempotent OK
        jmp ec_evt_ms_already;

      ec_evt_ms_store:
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  ec_evt_ms_same;              // NONE onto unset: nothing to do
        mov [r8 + 0], rsi;
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov rax, [r10 + 16];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 16], rax;             // installed
      ec_evt_ms_same:
        xor rax, rax;                    // EC_EVT_OK
        ret;

      ec_evt_ms_unbound:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDBF;             // EC_EVT_MAP_UNBOUND
        jmp ec_evt_ms_refused;
      ec_evt_ms_bad_byte:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDBE;             // EC_EVT_MAP_BAD_BYTE
        jmp ec_evt_ms_refused;
      ec_evt_ms_bad_class:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDBD;             // EC_EVT_MAP_BAD_CLASS
        jmp ec_evt_ms_refused;
      ec_evt_ms_already:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDBC;             // EC_EVT_MAP_ALREADY

      ec_evt_ms_refused:
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov r11, [r10 + 24];
        add r11, 1;
        mov [r10 + 24], r11;             // refused
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_class_of(ec_addr, query_byte) -> class
  //
  // THE LOOKUP. Never an error code: an unclassifiable byte is
  // EC_EVT_CLASS_NONE, and routing continues.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_class_of : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (ec_addr: u64, query_byte: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-004 (#1092): what this kernel believes query_byte means on controller ec_addr. RETURNS A CLASS AND NEVER AN ERROR CODE, EC_EVT_CLASS_NONE for every refusal -- unbound map, another controller's address, byte 0, byte >= 256, or a byte with no meaning installed. That is the opposite of this tree's usual habit and it is deliberate: the caller is the routing path, and the only thing it could do with an error is route the event anyway, which is what NONE already tells it to do. An unmapped byte MUST still be delivered: a subscriber that knows the platform can act on the byte itself, and withholding an event because the kernel could not label it would be the kernel censoring data it does not own. The ONE refusal that is counted is the FOREIGN one -- a lookup against a controller this map is not bound to -- because that is the only one that indicates a real configuration fault (a second embedded controller nobody installed meanings for) rather than a byte the vendor simply did not use, and sec 4 promises that a second controller gets no meanings rather than the wrong ones AND that the fact is observable. Index is query_byte << 3 into a table of whole qwords, so no entry shares a word with its neighbours; sec 5 is why. Leaf, no nested calls. Labels prefixed ec_evt_co_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov rax, [r10 + 0];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  ec_evt_co_none;

        mov rax, [r10 + 8];
        cmp rax, rdi;
        jne ec_evt_co_foreign;

        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  ec_evt_co_none;
        mov r11, 256;                    // EC_EVT_BYTE_MAX
        cmp rsi, r11;
        jae ec_evt_co_none;

        mov r8, rsi;
        shl r8, 3;
        lea r9, [rip + _ec_evt_map];
        add r8, r9;
        mov rax, [r8 + 0];
        ret;

      ec_evt_co_foreign:
        lea r10, [rip + _ec_evt_owner];
        mov rax, [r10 + 32];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [r10 + 32], rax;             // foreign
      ec_evt_co_none:
        xor rax, rax;                    // EC_EVT_CLASS_NONE
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_shed_policy(class) -> EC_EVT_SHED_ALWAYS
  //                              | EC_EVT_SHED_WATERMARK
  //                              | EC_EVT_SHED_UNDECLARED
  //
  // §7. TWO ENUMERATIONS AND A HOLE. Which set a class belongs to, decided
  // by name, one comparison per member, no ordering relation anywhere.
  //
  // Every declared class carries a `policy:` marker comment naming it.
  // tools/build.sh reads the declarations at the top of this file and
  // refuses the build if any of them is missing its marker here, so the
  // enumeration cannot grow without a decision being taken. The markers
  // are what that check reads; they are load-bearing, not decoration.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_shed_policy : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (class: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M2-002 (#1095): which admission set a query class belongs to. THIS FUNCTION EXISTS BECAUSE R31.M1 LEFT NON-SHEDDABILITY AS A DEFAULT AND R31.M2 ADDS A CLASS FOR WHICH A DEFAULT IS NOT GOOD ENOUGH. #1093 wrote the rule as a single inequality, `class != EC_EVT_CLASS_HOTKEY`, and defended it against a range compare on exactly the grounds that a range would silently sweep the thermal class into the watermark check the moment it was declared. That defence was right and it held: with the inequality, EC_EVT_CLASS_THERMAL is admitted unconditionally the instant it is declared and no line of the admission path has to change. But 'admitted because nothing named it' and 'admitted because it was named' are the same behaviour and different designs, and the difference is the NEXT class rather than this one. Thermal and battery are singletons, so the fail-open default happens to be right for both; R31.M4's platform sensors are not, and a sensor class that inherits unconditional admission by default would let a sample stream fill a 32-slot tail-drop ring that the power button shares -- the same priority inversion sec 7 was written to prevent, arriving from the other direction and past the very rule that prevents it. So BOTH sets are now enumerations: SHED_WATERMARK is {HOTKEY}, SHED_ALWAYS is {NONE, AC, LID, POWER, THERMAL}, each member reached by an equality against its own name, and nothing here is an ordering. A value in neither set answers SHED_UNDECLARED, which the caller treats as admit -- fail-safe at runtime, because refusing to admit an unrecognised class would drop precisely the safety event whose class somebody forgot to declare -- while tools/build.sh makes the hole UNREACHABLE for any DECLARED class by refusing a build whose policy body has no `policy:` marker for it. That is the guard: the runtime default is chosen to be harmless, and the build is what makes taking the default impossible. THE HOTKEY TEST COMES FIRST for no functional reason and one review reason: the sheddable set is the short one and the dangerous one, and a reader who stops after three lines has read the whole of it. Pure, no memory effect: the class is the only input and the answer is a property of the enumeration, not of the machine -- which is also why the depth check is NOT here. Leaf. Labels prefixed ec_evt_sp_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        mov r11, 4;                      // policy: HOTKEY
        cmp rdi, r11;
        je  ec_evt_sp_watermark;

        mov r11, 0;                      // policy: NONE
        cmp rdi, r11;
        je  ec_evt_sp_always;
        mov r11, 1;                      // policy: AC
        cmp rdi, r11;
        je  ec_evt_sp_always;
        mov r11, 2;                      // policy: LID
        cmp rdi, r11;
        je  ec_evt_sp_always;
        mov r11, 3;                      // policy: POWER
        cmp rdi, r11;
        je  ec_evt_sp_always;
        mov r11, 5;                      // policy: THERMAL
        cmp rdi, r11;
        je  ec_evt_sp_always;

        mov rax, 2;                      // EC_EVT_SHED_UNDECLARED
        ret;

      ec_evt_sp_watermark:
        mov rax, 1;                      // EC_EVT_SHED_WATERMARK
        ret;

      ec_evt_sp_always:
        xor rax, rax;                    // EC_EVT_SHED_ALWAYS
        ret
      }
    } @no_frame

  // ==========================================================================
  // ec_event_admit(class) -> 1 | 0
  //
  // §7. May a query of this class consume a stream slot right now.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let ec_event_admit : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (class: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R31.M1-005 (#1093), amended by R31.M2-002 (#1095): 1 iff a query of this class may be offered to the platform event stream now. The class-to-set decision is NOT taken here -- it is taken by ec_event_shed_policy, and this function is the two lines that turn a policy into an answer: SHED_WATERMARK classes are admitted only while acpi_evt_depth() < EC_EVT_HOTKEY_WATERMARK, every other answer is admitted outright. SPLITTING THE TWO IS THE #1095 CHANGE AND THE REASON IS A REVIEW PROPERTY, NOT A FUNCTIONAL ONE. #1093 wrote the rule as `class != EC_EVT_CLASS_HOTKEY` and defended it, correctly, against `class < EC_EVT_CLASS_HOTKEY` on the grounds that a range compare would sweep R31.M2 thermal into the watermark check the moment the class was declared. That defence held: EC_EVT_CLASS_THERMAL is admitted unconditionally and no line of the decision changed. But the inequality could only ever say which class IS sheddable, never which classes were CONSIDERED, so every future class was admitted by a default that nobody had to look at -- and R31.M4 platform sensors are a class for which that default is wrong in the other direction, a sample stream quietly consuming a 32-slot tail-drop ring the power button shares. Both sets are enumerations now, and tools/build.sh refuses a build in which a declared class is in neither. WHAT THIS FUNCTION STILL OWNS: the stream depth. The policy is a property of the class and is pure; whether a slot is available is a property of the machine at this instant, and keeping the two apart is what lets a witness assert the policy of a class without a stream in any particular state. THE INVERSION THIS PREVENTS, unchanged from #1093: Fn keys arrive in bursts, the ring is tail-drop, so a long enough burst makes the NEXT record refused be whatever arrives next -- including the power button, whose loss the user answers by holding the button, i.e. by an unclean shutdown. The watermark reserves the top eight slots against that. The reservation is SOFT and stated as such in sec 7: it holds against hot-keys only, since GPE and firmware-notification records share this stream and carry no class, so a GPE storm can still fill the ring -- which the per-class `lost` counter then records rather than hides. A predicate and not a counting function: the shed is counted by the caller, at the point where it decides to shed, so that a future caller which asks the question without acting on it does not move a counter that means an event was dropped. One push (rbx, carrying the class) leaves rsp%16 == 0 at both nested calls. Labels prefixed ec_evt_ad_ per reserved-label discipline.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        mov rbx, rdi;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        call ec_event_shed_policy;
        mov r11, 1;                      // EC_EVT_SHED_WATERMARK
        cmp rax, r11;
        jne ec_evt_ad_yes;               // ALWAYS and UNDECLARED both admit

        call acpi_evt_depth;
        mov r11, 24;                     // EC_EVT_HOTKEY_WATERMARK
        cmp rax, r11;
        jae ec_evt_ad_no;

      ec_evt_ad_yes:
        mov rax, 1;
        pop rbx;
        ret;

      ec_evt_ad_no:
        xor rax, rax;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }
}
